# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored — never commit real values.

# --- Local development ------------------------------------------------------
PORT=6686
STORAGE_PATH=./storage
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://routeloop:routeloop_dev_pw@127.0.0.1:5432/routeloop


# Google Maps, browser side. HTTP-referrer restricted and API-restricted to
# Maps JavaScript + Places (New). This ships in page source — it is public by
# design, and the referrer list is the only thing controlling who can spend it.
# Referrers: routeloop.app/*, stage.routeloop.app/*, localhost:6686/*
GMAPS_KEY=

# Google Maps, server side. IP-restricted to the origin's egress address and
# API-restricted to Routes + Geocoding + Static Maps. Never send this to a
# client; it must not appear in page(), window.TB, or any JSON response.
#
# The two are NOT interchangeable. Referrer restrictions need a Referer header,
# which servers do not send, and IP restrictions cannot work from a browser —
# so one key physically cannot do both jobs.
#
# Leave blank while every Maps call is made from the browser; the deploy warns
# but does not fail. Note that IP restriction assumes a static egress IP: on a
# residential connection an IP change silently breaks server-side calls while
# the browser key keeps working.
GMAPS_SERVER_KEY=

# A Cloud-based map style id (Google Maps Platform -> Map management). Advanced
# Markers do not render without one — they fail with a console warning and no
# visible marker, which reads as a data bug rather than a config one.
GMAPS_MAP_ID=

# --- Alpha splash -----------------------------------------------------------
# Links in the "this is an alpha" modal. These are public URLs, not secrets, and
# src/config.ts already defaults them to the live values — set them here only to
# override. An empty value omits that one link rather than rendering a dead one.
# ALPHA_GITHUB_URL=https://github.com/feralcreative/routeloop/issues
# ALPHA_SIGNAL_URL=https://feral.ly/signal
# ALPHA_DISCORD_URL=https://discord.gg/5wqFRxqzxN
# ALPHA_VMC_URL=https://vampiresmc.com

# --- Auth -------------------------------------------------------------------
# APP_ORIGIN is the public origin of THIS environment. It builds the OAuth
# redirect URI, backs the CSRF gate, and decides whether session cookies get the
# Secure flag — so an https value is required in stage and prod.
#   dev   http://127.0.0.1:6686
#   stage https://stage.routeloop.app
#   prod  https://routeloop.app
APP_ORIGIN=http://127.0.0.1:6686

# Sign in with Google. The consent screen must request exactly
# openid + email + profile: anything sensitive or restricted drags this client
# through Google's verification review and caps the app at 100 users. The
# redirect URI is derived as <APP_ORIGIN>/auth/google/callback and must be listed
# on the client. See docs/google-cloud-setup.md.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Outbound mail. Deliberately a SEPARATE credential from the OAuth client above —
# mail is sent by the server from its own address, never as the signed-in user,
# which is what keeps a Gmail scope off the consent screen. See docs/email.md.
#
# Resend over plain SMTP, so changing provider is a change to these five values
# and nothing else. SMTP_USER is the literal string `resend` — not an address —
# and SMTP_PASS is the API key. The domain must be verified in Resend first, and
# its SPF/DKIM records live in Cloudflare DNS.
#
# MAIL_FROM must be a BARE address, not `Name <addr>`: the display name is added
# in mailer.ts, and the bracketed form has to survive printf into a compose .env
# where quoting rules differ and a `#` starts a comment.
#
# Leave all of it blank and the app still runs on Google sign-in alone — but the
# email sign-in form is hidden AND no notification is sent at all: no waitlist
# confirmation, no approval notice, no owner alert.
SMTP_HOST=smtp.resend.com
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=resend
SMTP_PASS=
MAIL_FROM=

# Google authenticates; users.status authorizes. Every new account is created
# 'pending' and sees only /welcome until it is approved — except this one, which
# is always created 'active'. Both databases have been rebuilt from empty
# before, and without this the owner would come back pending with nobody able to
# approve them. Defaults to ziad@feralcreative.co in src/config.ts.
# OWNER_EMAIL=ziad@feralcreative.co

# --- Account purge (DESTRUCTIVE) ---------------------------------------------
# Off unless set to exactly `on`. When on, the hourly sweep in src/account/purge.ts
# destroys any account whose 30-day GTFO hold has run out — the account, every ride
# it owns, and every file it uploaded, none of it recoverable outside a backup.
#
# It ships off because /account/delete has been promising riders a destruction date
# since it shipped and nothing ever kept it, so the first pass finds everyone who is
# already overdue and takes them all at once. Run
# `npx tsx utils/purge-accounts.ts --dry-run` and read the list before turning this
# on, and take a db-backup either way. Any other value, `false` included, leaves it off.
# PURGE_ACCOUNTS=on

# How long a container that has been told to stop waits for its in-flight
# requests before cutting them off, in milliseconds. Written into the deployed
# .env by utils/deploy/deploy.sh; the default is what src/config.ts falls back
# to, so a local run never needs it. Keep it comfortably under the compose
# service's stop_grace_period (30s) or the runtime kills the drain part-way and
# this number reads as though it did something.
# DRAIN_GRACE_MS=10000

# --- Turnstile (bot check on the file upload) --------------------------------
# Cloudflare Turnstile is a CAPTCHA replacement: the browser gets a one-time
# token from Cloudflare's widget, the server posts it to siteverify, and the
# upload is refused if it does not check out. See src/maps/turnstile.ts, which
# fails CLOSED — if Cloudflare is unreachable the upload is rejected, not waved
# through.
#
# THE SECRET IS THE SWITCH. Set it and enforcement turns on; leave it blank and
# every check passes. It guards ONE endpoint, POST /api/maps — the file upload,
# which parses whatever a stranger hands it and writes to disk against a quota.
# Ride creation deliberately has no such gate; see the note above
# POST /api/rides in src/routes/builder.ts for why.
#
# The site key is public and ships in page source; only the secret is a secret.
#
# ORDER MATTERS AND GETTING IT WRONG BREAKS CI. These are NOT yet in
# REMOTE_ENV_KEYS in utils/deploy/deploy.sh, and must not be added until the
# values exist on every server: that list is a hard requirement, so a key named
# there but absent from the server's .env fails the deploy outright. Create the
# widget, fill these in, run `utils/deploy/deploy-utils.sh push-env` for each
# environment, and only THEN add them to the allow-list and to the
# `environment:` block of BOTH color services in docker-compose.prod.yml. A key
# on the allow-list with no compose line is read by nobody.
# TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=

# --- Dev sign-in (LOCAL ONLY) ------------------------------------------------
# Set this and GET http://127.0.0.1:6686/dev/login signs you in as that account,
# no password and no emailed link. It saves minting a session token by hand every
# time you want to look at /builder or /welcome.
#
# It is off unless ALL of these hold, and when off the route is not registered at
# all — /dev/login is a plain 404, not a refusal:
#
#   1. DEV_LOGIN_EMAIL is set to an address that already has an account. It will
#      not create one.
#   2. DATABASE_URL points at 127.0.0.1, localhost or host.docker.internal.
#   3. APP_ORIGIN is not https.
#   4. The request's Host is 127.0.0.1 or localhost — not the LAN address.
#
# Safe to leave set. The deploy writes the server's .env from an explicit
# allow-list in utils/deploy/deploy.sh, and this is not on it — so it cannot
# reach a server through a deploy, and the script asserts that of the file it is
# about to send.
# DEV_LOGIN_EMAIL=ziad@feralcreative.co

# --- Deploy: NAS database passwords -----------------------------------------
# Required by utils/deploy/deploy.sh for the matching environment.
#
# NOTE: Postgres only reads this when it initializes its data volume on the
# FIRST deploy. Changing it later does not change the database password — you
# must ALTER USER inside the running db container (or drop the volume).
#
# Use alphanumerics only: the value is interpolated into a postgresql:// URL,
# so characters like @ : / # would corrupt the connection string.
PROD_DB_PASSWORD=
STAGE_DB_PASSWORD=

# --- Deploy: the container registry -----------------------------------------
#
# The image is pushed to GHCR and PULLED by the NAS — it is no longer piped over
# the SSH connection. See IMAGE_REPO in deploy.config.
#
# BOTH TOKENS ARE OPTIONAL, AND WHICH ONES YOU NEED DEPENDS ON ONE SETTING IN
# GITHUB rather than on anything here. A GHCR package created by a first push is
# PRIVATE even when its repository is public:
#
#   package private  →  GHCR_TOKEN to push, NAS_GHCR_TOKEN to pull
#   package public   →  GHCR_TOKEN to push, and the NAS needs nothing
#
# Leave a token unset and that login is skipped, so making the package public
# later is a change in GitHub's UI and a line deleted here — not a code change.
#
# GHCR_TOKEN is a classic PAT with `write:packages`. NAS_GHCR_TOKEN should be a
# SEPARATE token with `read:packages` and nothing else: it lives on the NAS, and
# a read-only credential that leaks cannot publish an image everything trusts.
#
# You can also skip GHCR_TOKEN entirely and `docker login ghcr.io` by hand once;
# the push uses whatever credentials Docker already holds.
# GHCR_USER=
# GHCR_TOKEN=
# NAS_GHCR_USER=
# NAS_GHCR_TOKEN=

# --- Deploy: Cloudflare cache purge (optional but recommended) ---------------
# Token needs Zone.Cache Purge on the routeloop.app zone.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID=
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=

# --- Deploy: SSH ------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional override; default auto-detects ~/.ssh/id_ed25519, then id_rsa, then
# ssh-agent (1Password).
# SSH_KEY_PATH=

# --- Build stamp -------------------------------------------------------------
# Both are SET BY THE DEPLOY, not by you. utils/deploy/deploy.sh computes them
# from the commit being shipped and writes them into the server's .env via its
# allow-list. Leave them unset locally: the app falls back to "dev", which is the
# honest answer for a build nobody deployed.
#
# APP_VERSION is YYYY-MM-DD-HHMMPT — the minute the commit was made, Pacific. It
# is what a rider sees in the footer and in the release-notes dialog, so it is
# the string a beta tester will quote in a report. The zone is in the value on
# purpose: a bare timestamp gets read in the reader's own clock.
# APP_VERSION=
# BUILD_SHA=
